module postgres::pool;

//! Several connections, and a fair way to take one.
//!
//! **The pool is a queue holding the idle connections.** Taking one is a
//! receive, giving it back is a send, and waiting when they are all busy is
//! what a queue already does. There is no free list, no condition variable and
//! no count of who has what -- those are the parts a pool usually is, and they
//! are all the queue's.
//!
//! # The connection itself is lent
//!
//! The queue is a `Handoff`, not a `Channel`, and what it carries is the
//! connection. A borrower receives it, drives it on its own fiber through
//! `postgres::db::direct`, and sends it back when its lease ends. Nothing
//! stands between the borrower and the socket: no serving fiber, no request
//! channel, no reply channel.
//!
//! **What makes that sound is the send.** A `Handoff` send gives the value
//! away: it checks, at the send, that nothing outside the value holds any
//! writable part of it, and traps otherwise, so the connection is never
//! reachable from two fibers. The pool keeps every reference to a connection
//! inside the `Loan` it travels in, and the give-back empties the lease's
//! holder before it sends, so that check passes.
//!
//! **A lease's `db` is the lease's fiber's alone.** The `Db` a lease installs
//! drives the connection on the fiber that took the lease; the checker keeps
//! it there, and a fiber spawned inside the body that needs the database takes
//! a lease of its own.
//!
//! # Backpressure comes for free, and that is the point
//!
//! A pool of four with five fibers wanting a connection does not queue the
//! fifth request against the database and it does not fail it. The fifth
//! *fiber* waits, having given its worker back, until somebody finishes. Under
//! more load than the pool can serve, the waiting happens at the edge -- which
//! is what stops a service from accepting work until it runs out of memory.
//!
//! # Leases, capabilities, and the region they belong to
//!
//! `with_db` takes a connection, installs it as `db: Db` for the body, and must
//! give it back including when the body raises and including when the fiber
//! running it is canceled or aborted. Application code therefore keeps `Db` in
//! its capability row instead of accepting a connection value just because the
//! provider happens to be a pool.
//!
//! Returning the lease is a region's job, and three rules keep a lease from
//! being lost. Each was learned from a pool that lost one:
//!
//! - **The give-back is registered before the first cancellation point after
//!   the receive.** A receive that returns a value has handed the connection
//!   to this fiber, and from then on only a registered finalizer can bring it
//!   back. `Region::open` and `Region::defer` are not cancellation points, so
//!   nothing lies between the receive and the registration. Registered
//!   through `scoped` or `acquire`, each entered through a cancellation check,
//!   a fiber canceled as it was handed a connection unwound holding it: a pool
//!   of one lost its only connection within two hundred such cancels, and
//!   then `with_db` and `close` both waited for ever.
//! - **The give-back is built in a function that calls no function value**,
//!   so it has no entry poll and an `abort` cannot skip it. See `returner`.
//! - **The give-back's send cannot wait.** `idle` has room for every
//!   connection and the down token, and the one going back is not in it. An
//!   aborted fiber does not wait for room, so a send that could wait could be
//!   dropped, and the connection with it.
//!
//! **The region has to be `with_db`'s own.** Taken from a caller's `Scope`,
//! the lease is held until the *caller's* region ends rather than until the
//! call returns: four jobs against a pool of two, all inside one `scoped`,
//! took both connections and gave neither back, and the third waited for
//! ever. So `with_db` opens a region per lease. A caller needs no `Scope`
//! capability for it, and the `Db` requirement disappears at this boundary
//! because the leased handler is installed here.
//!
//! **The transaction is covered too**: `std::db::transaction` registers its
//! rollback with a region of its own, so a fiber stopped mid-flight sends the
//! `ROLLBACK` on its way out, and the lease then goes back holding a
//! connection that is ready. A connection a stopped borrower left with a
//! reply still arriving goes to its slot, which reads the rest and lends it
//! again. One left inside a transaction, or broken part-way through a reply,
//! is not lent again: its slot closes and reconnects it.
//!
//! # Checked before every lease, on the borrower's fiber
//!
//! **A connection that is down is never lent.** Before the body runs,
//! `with_db` asks the connection it received whether its socket is still open
//! -- a server restart closes idle connections, and nothing reads an idle
//! socket to notice -- and whether it is outside a transaction. One that
//! fails is marked broken, given back to its slot to be reconnected, and
//! `with_db` takes the next offer. What it costs: one non-blocking read per
//! lease. What it does not see: a connection that dies between the check and
//! the borrower's first statement. That statement is answered
//! `Disconnected`, and the slot reconnects when the lease ends.
//!
//! # Reconnecting, shrinking, and growing back
//!
//! **Each slot has a fiber that owns its connection's opening and closing**,
//! though not its use. It opens the connection, puts it into `idle`, and
//! waits for it to come home: a lease that ends with the connection broken
//! sends it to its slot rather than to `idle`.
//!
//! - A slot's connection is in `idle` only while it is connected. `open` puts
//!   none there; each slot's fiber puts its own there once it opens.
//! - Reconnecting closes the old socket first and never reads it again, then
//!   tries on the pool's fast `Schedule`. Meanwhile the slot counts as
//!   `reconnecting`, and callers wait for it.
//! - A connection that breaks before any lease on it has passed the check
//!   counts as a failed attempt, so a server that accepts connections and
//!   spoils each one is backed off like a server that refuses them.
//! - When the fast schedule gives up, the slot **shrinks out**: it counts as
//!   `down`, and tries again on the slow interval until it connects (and
//!   rejoins `idle`) or the pool closes.
//! - **A pool with no slot live or reconnecting fails its callers at once**,
//!   with the reason the last attempt failed. The callers already waiting are
//!   woken by the down token (`Lendable::Down`), put into `idle` when the last
//!   slot shrinks. A caller that receives it answers `Disconnected`, and its
//!   give-back puts the token back for the next one; once a slot is live or
//!   reconnecting again, the next caller to receive it drops it.

import std::core::{Changed, Fiber, Fibers, Handoff, List, Option, Region, Result, Shared};
import std::clock::{Clock};
import std::db::{Db, DbError};
import std::random::{Random};
import std::resilience::{Schedule};
import postgres::conn::{Connection, forsake, in_transaction, mid_reply, settle, still_open, usable};
import postgres::db::{Settings, connect, direct, hang_up};

/// A set of connections, each with a slot that opens and reopens it.
pub type Pool = {
  /// What is ready to lend: each connection that is open and unused, as a
  /// `Loan` -- plus, at most once, the down token. The module doc has both.
  idle: Handoff<Lendable>,
  /// Whether the down token is in `idle`. With each slot's own `here`, this
  /// is how `idle_count` counts what `idle` holds: a `Handoff` has no depth.
  token_idle: Shared<Bool>,
  /// Each slot's `here`, for `idle_count`.
  slots: List<Shared<Bool>>,
  /// The slots' fibers.
  ///
  /// **Held here because a pool that did not hold them would stop working on
  /// its way out of `open`.** A caller with no later use for its nursery hands
  /// over its last reference, so the nursery is released when `open` returns --
  /// and releasing a nursery cancels its children and waits for them, which is
  /// every slot this just started. The symptom is a pool that hangs before it
  /// is ever used, and the cause is two rooms away.
  crew: Fibers,
  /// How many slots are in each state, the last reason a connection failed,
  /// and whether the pool is closing.
  tally: Shared<Tally>,
};

/// How a pool reconnects a connection it has lost.
///
/// Every field `pub`: this is configuration a caller writes and hands to
/// `open_with`, and `default` and `never` are two starting points for it.
pub type Reconnect = {
  /// The fast phase: when to try again after a failure, and when to give up.
  /// A slot whose schedule gives up shrinks out of the pool.
  pub fast: Schedule,
  /// The slow phase: how often, in milliseconds, a slot that shrank out
  /// tries again, with 80-120% jitter. `None` makes the shrink permanent.
  pub slow: Option<Int>,
  /// How long, in milliseconds, a connection attempt waits for the server to
  /// finish its handshake before it counts as failed and the schedule goes
  /// on. Without it, a server that accepts and never answers held the slot,
  /// its waiting callers, and `close` for ever. The TCP connect before the
  /// handshake is not covered: see "What it cannot cut short" on `close`.
  pub handshake: Int,
};

impl Reconnect {
  /// Exponential backoff from 50 ms, doubling to a 5 s cap, with 50-100%
  /// jitter, for at most 30 s; then a try every 30 s. A handshake gets 10 s.
  ///
  /// **Why these numbers.** The fast phase is for the ordinary bad minute --
  /// a server restart, a failover, a dropped route -- and callers wait
  /// through it, so its total is a caller's patience. 30 s is HikariCP's
  /// default `connectionTimeout`, how long a borrower there waits before it
  /// is failed. The backoff is HikariCP's own shape for creating connections
  /// (10 ms doubling to a 5 s cap); 50 ms first still reaches a server that
  /// was only restarting on the first or second try, and the 5 s cap keeps a
  /// pool of 20 from making 20 connection attempts a second at a server that
  /// is down. The jitter spreads a fleet's retries apart after an outage
  /// they all saw at once.
  ///
  /// The slow phase is why a pool shrunk to nothing grows back without a
  /// restart. HikariCP keeps creating connections up to its minimum, which is
  /// the pool size by default, and its housekeeper runs every 30 s; 30 s is
  /// that. Other pools do less: pgx refills in the background only up to
  /// `MinConns`, which is zero unless set, and deadpool and node-postgres
  /// create a connection only when a caller asks, so their callers meet the
  /// connect error themselves. A slot here costs one attempt per 30 s
  /// against a server that is down.
  ///
  /// 10 s for a handshake is pgJDBC's default `connectTimeout`, and ten times
  /// what a healthy server needs even over a slow link. libpq's
  /// `connect_timeout` and pgx's `ConnectTimeout` default to no bound at all,
  /// which is the hang this field exists to rule out.
  pub fn default() -> Reconnect {
    {
      fast: Schedule::UpTo(Schedule::backoff(50, 5000), 30000), slow: Option::Some(30000),
      handshake: 10000,
    }
  }

  /// One attempt, then shrink for good.
  pub fn never() -> Reconnect {
    { fast: Schedule::once(), slow: Option::None, handshake: 10000 }
  }
}

/// What travels through `idle`.
///
/// **The down token is a case of its own, not a connection that looks like
/// one**, so nothing a borrower does to a connection can make it pass for the
/// token, or the token for a connection.
type Lendable =
  | Ready(Loan)
  /// There is nothing to lend: no slot is live or reconnecting.
  | Down;

/// One connection, and everything its lease needs to send it back.
///
/// **Every reference to the connection is in here**, and `Handoff::send`
/// checks that at every send: this record is what the send gives away, so a
/// field anywhere else that held `c` would make that send trap.
type Loan = {
  c: Connection,
  /// Where the connection goes when a lease finds it broken: its slot's
  /// fiber, which closes and reopens it.
  home: Handoff<Lendable>,
  /// Whether this connection is in `idle` right now, for `idle_count`.
  here: Shared<Bool>,
  /// Whether any lease on this connection passed its check. A connection
  /// that breaks before one has counts as a failed connection attempt.
  mut served: Bool,
};

/// Where a lease keeps what it received, so its give-back can take it out.
///
/// **What this prevents: a give-back that traps because the lease still holds
/// the connection.** The finalizer captures this holder, not the loan, and
/// empties it before it sends, so the loan in hand is the only reference.
type Held = { mut got: Option<Lendable> };

/// How many slots are in each state. See `health`.
///
/// Every field `pub`: this is a report, read by whoever asked `health`, and
/// building one proves nothing about any pool.
pub type Health = {
  /// Connected: idle, or lent to somebody.
  pub live: Int,
  /// Lost, and being reconnected on the fast schedule. Callers wait for
  /// these.
  pub reconnecting: Int,
  /// Shrunk out: the fast schedule gave up. Retrying on the slow interval,
  /// if there is one, and never lent until a retry succeeds.
  pub down: Int,
};

/// The pool's shared bookkeeping, changed only under its lock.
type Tally = {
  live: Int,
  reconnecting: Int,
  down: Int,
  /// Why the last connection attempt failed.
  last: String,
  closing: Bool,
  /// Whether the down token is out, so there is never more than one.
  token: Bool,
  /// How many slots' fibers have not finished. `close` gathers loans until
  /// this is 0.
  running: Int,
};

/// Where a slot is.
type Phase =
  | Live
  | Reconnecting
  | Down;

/// How far a slot is into its reconnect schedule: when the run of failures
/// began, how many attempts have failed, and which phase that has put it in.
///
/// **Kept across connections, and started again only when a connection has
/// passed a check.** A server that finishes the handshake and then spoils
/// every connection before it can be lent -- a backend terminated as soon as
/// it is admitted, a proxy in front of a server that is down -- let every
/// connect succeed, and a schedule that started again at each success never
/// counted one failure: one slot opened hundreds of connections a second,
/// and on macOS ran the loopback out of ports until `connect` itself failed.
type Tries = {
  mut began: Int,
  mut attempts: Int,
  mut phase: Phase,
};

/// How a connection's lending ended.
type Ended =
  /// The pool is closing.
  | Closing
  /// It broke after at least one lease had passed its check.
  | Served
  /// It broke without a lease ever passing its check: a failed attempt.
  | Spoiled;

/// One try at a lease.
type Tried<A> =
  | Done(A)
  /// The connection failed its check and has been sent home; take another.
  | Again
  | Refused(DbError);

/// Opens a pool of `count` connections that reconnects with
/// `Reconnect::default()`.
pub fn open(crew: Fibers, settings: Settings, count: Int) -> Pool {
  open_with(crew, settings, count, Reconnect::default())
}

/// Opens `count` slots and adopts a fiber for each into `crew`.
///
/// **Returns before any of them has connected.** A slot's fiber opens its
/// own connection, so this is a spawn and not a round trip. `with_db` waits
/// for the first connection to open; a pool that cannot connect at all
/// answers `Disconnected` with the reason once every slot's fast schedule has
/// given up.
///
/// Below one connection is one.
pub fn open_with(crew: Fibers, settings: Settings, count: Int, plan: Reconnect) -> Pool {
  let wanted = if count < 1 { 1 } else { count };
  // One more than the slots, for the down token: there is never more than
  // one connection per slot and one token, so a send here never waits.
  let idle: Handoff<Lendable> = Handoff::bounded(wanted + 1);
  let token_idle = Shared::of(false);
  let tally = Shared::of({
      live: 0, reconnecting: wanted, down: 0, last: "no connection has been tried yet",
      closing: false, token: false, running: wanted,
    });
  let mut slots: List<Shared<Bool>> = List::Nil;
  let mut made = 0;
  while made < wanted {
    let here = Shared::of(false);
    slots = List::Cons(here, slots);
    Fibers::adopt(crew, Fiber::spawn(fn () => slot(settings, plan, idle, token_idle, here, tally)));
    made = made + 1
  };
  { idle: idle, token_idle: token_idle, slots: slots, crew: crew, tally: tally }
}

/// How many slots are live, reconnecting, and shrunk out, right now.
///
/// For tests and health checks. A pool with none live or reconnecting answers
/// every `with_db` with `Disconnected` at once.
pub fn health(pool: Pool) -> Health {
  let t = Shared::get(pool.tally);
  { live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down }
}

/// How many offers are waiting to be taken: connections nobody has borrowed,
/// plus the down token if it is out.
///
/// Stale the moment it is given, as every such count is. For tests and
/// health checks, which wait for it to reach a number; nothing should branch
/// on it. What it costs: one look at each slot's flag.
pub fn idle_count(pool: Pool) -> Int {
  let mut n = if Shared::get(pool.token_idle) { 1 } else { 0 };
  let mut rest = pool.slots;
  let mut going = true;
  while going {
    match rest {
      List::Nil => going = false,
      List::Cons(here, tail) => {
        if Shared::get(here) { n = n + 1 };
        rest = tail
      },
    }
  };
  n
}

/// Runs `body` with a leased connection installed as the `db: Db` capability.
///
/// Waits while every connection is busy or reconnecting. The connection goes
/// back **when this call returns**, however it returns -- normally, raising,
/// canceled or aborted -- because the lease is registered with a region this
/// function opens, before anything after the receive can be canceled.
///
/// **The connection is checked before the body runs**, on this fiber: one
/// whose socket the server has closed, or that is inside a transaction, is
/// sent to its slot to be reconnected and never lent, and the next one is
/// taken.
///
/// **Answers `Err(Disconnected(reason))` at once when no connection is live
/// or reconnecting**, with the reason the last attempt failed, rather than
/// waiting for a slow retry that may be half a minute away.
///
/// The `db` requirement is handled here and removed from the caller's row. Any
/// other requirements and raised failures on `body` pass straight through.
/// The `Result` is only about obtaining the lease. The `db` the body is given
/// stays on this fiber: a fiber the body spawns takes a lease of its own.
pub fn with_db<A, 'ef, 'er>(
  pool: Pool,
  body: () -> A with { 'ef | db: Db } raises 'er,
) -> Result<A, DbError>
  with 'ef
  raises 'er
{
  // **A loop, not a call to itself.** A slot whose server finishes the
  // handshake and then fails every check offers a new connection every few
  // milliseconds; taking each by a call to `with_db` cost a frame apiece,
  // and a caller that waited through a few seconds of that ran out of stack.
  let mut tried = one_try(pool, body)!;
  while again(tried) { tried = one_try(pool, body)! };
  match tried {
    Tried::Done(answer) => Result::Ok(answer),
    // Not reached: the loop leaves only on `Done` or `Refused`.
    Tried::Again => Result::Err(DbError::Disconnected("the pool is closed")),
    Tried::Refused(why) => Result::Err(why),
  }
}

fn again<A>(tried: Tried<A>) -> Bool {
  match tried { Tried::Again => true, Tried::Done(_) => false, Tried::Refused(_) => false }
}

/// One offer from `idle` and its lease, which ends when this returns.
fn one_try<A, 'ef, 'er>(
  pool: Pool,
  body: () -> A with { 'ef | db: Db } raises 'er,
) -> Tried<A>
  with 'ef
  raises 'er
{
  // **Received straight into the holder, with no binding between.** The
  // holder is then the only thing on this fiber that holds the offer, which
  // is what lets the give-back take it out and send it on.
  let held = holding(Handoff::receive(pool.idle));
  // **The give-back is registered before anything in this function can
  // poll**; see the module doc. The region is this call's, so the lease ends
  // when it returns: before a connection that failed its check is replaced by
  // another, not after.
  let lease = Region::open();
  Region::defer(lease, returner(pool.idle, pool.token_idle, pool.tally, held));
  match what(held, pool.token_idle) {
    Offered::Nothing => Tried::Refused(DbError::Disconnected("the pool is closed")),
    Offered::Token => answer_down(pool),
    Offered::Lent => {
      if fit(held) {
        Tried::Done(run(held, body)!)
      } else {
        Tried::Again
      }
    },
  }
}

/// What a lease received, without holding any of it.
type Offered =
  | Nothing
  | Token
  | Lent;

fn holding(got: Option<Lendable>) -> Held {
  { got: got }
}

/// What `held` holds, and marks it as out of `idle`.
fn what(held: Held, token_idle: Shared<Bool>) -> Offered {
  match held.got {
    Option::None => Offered::Nothing,
    Option::Some(Lendable::Down) => {
      Shared::set(token_idle, false);
      Offered::Token
    },
    Option::Some(Lendable::Ready(loan)) => {
      Shared::set(loan.here, false);
      Offered::Lent
    },
  }
}

/// Whether the connection `held` holds can be lent: the check P-5 asks for,
/// made on the borrower's fiber. One that fails is marked broken, so its
/// give-back sends it home to be reconnected; one that passes marks its loan
/// served, so a later break is not taken for a failed connection attempt.
fn fit(held: Held) -> Bool {
  match held.got {
    Option::Some(Lendable::Ready(loan)) =>
      if !mid_reply(loan.c) && still_open(loan.c) && !in_transaction(loan.c) {
        loan.served = true;
        true
      } else {
        forsake(loan.c);
        false
      },
    Option::Some(Lendable::Down) => false,
    Option::None => false,
  }
}

/// Runs `body` over the connection `held` holds.
fn run<A, 'ef, 'er>(held: Held, body: () -> A with { 'ef | db: Db } raises 'er) -> A
  with 'ef
  raises 'er
{
  match held.got {
    Option::Some(Lendable::Ready(loan)) => body()! with { db: direct(loan.c) },
    // Not reached: `one_try` runs this only for a connection, and only the
    // give-back empties the holder.
    Option::Some(Lendable::Down) => body()! with { db: refusing() },
    Option::None => body()! with { db: refusing() },
  }
}

/// A `Db` that answers everything `Disconnected`. Installed only on paths
/// `one_try` does not take; a lease never runs a body without a connection.
fn refusing() -> Db {
  handler for Db {
    query: fn (_sql, _values) => Result::Err(nothing_lent()),
    execute: fn (_sql, _values) => Result::Err(nothing_lent()),
    begin: fn () => Result::Err(nothing_lent()),
    commit: fn () => Result::Err(nothing_lent()),
    rollback: fn () => Result::Err(nothing_lent()),
    depth: fn () => 0,
    savepoint: fn _level => Result::Err(nothing_lent()),
    release: fn _level => Result::Err(nothing_lent()),
    rollback_to: fn _level => Result::Err(nothing_lent()),
    broken: fn () => (),
    query_each: fn (_sql, sets) => List::map(sets, fn _values => Result::Err(nothing_lent())),
  }
}

fn nothing_lent() -> DbError {
  DbError::Disconnected("no connection was lent")
}

/// A caller was handed the down token: the reason, or another try if a
/// connection has come back since the token was made.
///
/// **Only decides; the lease's give-back does the passing on.** Sending the
/// token back to `idle` from here would come after several cancellation
/// points, and a caller stopped before the send would unwind holding it:
/// `tally` would still say a token was out, so no other would ever be made,
/// and every later `with_db` would wait for ever. The give-back re-offers it
/// under the rule this leaves in `tally.token`, and it runs however the
/// caller stops.
///
/// A closing pool keeps the token, so every caller waiting in `with_db` is
/// woken and answered.
fn answer_down<A>(pool: Pool) -> Tried<A> {
  let keep = Shared::modify(pool.tally, fn t => {
      let down = t.closing || t.live + t.reconnecting == 0;
      {
        state: { live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down, last: t.last,
          closing: t.closing, token: down, running: t.running },
        result: down,
      }
    });
  let t = Shared::get(pool.tally);
  if !keep {
    Tried::Again
  } else if t.closing {
    Tried::Refused(DbError::Disconnected("the pool is closed"))
  } else {
    Tried::Refused(DbError::Disconnected(t.last))
  }
}

/// The give-back `with_db` registers, built outside `with_db`.
///
/// **What this prevents: an `abort` losing the lease without running a line
/// of the give-back.** A lambda written inside a function that calls a
/// function value polls when it is entered, because it may be the far end of
/// that call. `with_db` calls `body`, so a give-back written inline there
/// polled first, and an `abort` -- which stops cleanup at its next
/// cancellation point -- landing while a transaction's `ROLLBACK` waited
/// stopped it at that poll. The give-back never ran, the pool was one
/// connection short for good, and at none every later `with_db` and `close`
/// waited for ever. Built here, in a function that calls no function value,
/// the lambda has no entry poll.
fn returner(idle: Handoff<Lendable>, token_idle: Shared<Bool>, tally: Shared<Tally>, held: Held) -> () -> () {
  fn () => give_back(idle, token_idle, tally, held)
}

/// Ends a lease, in one step that cannot wait and cannot fail.
///
/// **Takes what the lease holds out of its holder first**, so the value in
/// hand is the only reference to it and the send can give it away.
///
/// A connection goes back to `idle` if it is fit for the next borrower and
/// the pool is open; otherwise to its slot, which closes it and, unless the
/// pool is closing, reconnects. Neither send can wait: `idle` has room for
/// every connection and one token, and a slot's `home` has room for its one
/// connection; the one being sent is in neither.
///
/// The down token goes back into `idle` for the next caller while the pool
/// has nothing live or reconnecting, or is closing -- the rule `answer_down`
/// applies, and `tally.token` is where it left its decision.
///
/// **Whether it is the token is fixed by what was received, and never learned
/// by trying a send.** A send that fails is followed by a cancellation check,
/// and an aborted caller stops at it, so a give-back that branched on a
/// send's answer never got as far as acting on it.
fn give_back(idle: Handoff<Lendable>, token_idle: Shared<Bool>, tally: Shared<Tally>, held: Held) -> () {
  let got = take_out(held);
  match kind_of(got) {
    Offered::Nothing => (),
    Offered::Token =>
      if Shared::get(tally).token {
        Shared::set(token_idle, true);
        Handoff::send(idle, Lendable::Down);
        ()
      },
    Offered::Lent =>
      if lendable(got) && !Shared::get(tally).closing {
        mark_idle(got);
        Handoff::send(idle, the_offer(got));
        ()
      } else {
        send_home(the_offer(got))
      },
  }
}

/// Empties the holder and answers what it held.
fn take_out(held: Held) -> Option<Lendable> {
  let was = held.got;
  held.got = Option::None;
  was
}

fn kind_of(got: Option<Lendable>) -> Offered {
  match got {
    Option::None => Offered::Nothing,
    Option::Some(Lendable::Down) => Offered::Token,
    Option::Some(Lendable::Ready(_)) => Offered::Lent,
  }
}

/// Whether the next borrower can have this connection from `idle` straight
/// away: nothing has broken it, no reply is still arriving on it, and it is
/// outside a transaction. A connection an `abort` left inside one would run
/// the next borrower's statements in it; one left mid-reply would hand them
/// the stopped caller's answer. Either goes to its slot instead, which reads
/// what is still arriving and closes what cannot be lent, because the
/// give-back must not wait.
fn lendable(got: Option<Lendable>) -> Bool {
  match got {
    Option::Some(Lendable::Ready(loan)) =>
      usable(loan.c) && !mid_reply(loan.c) && !in_transaction(loan.c),
    Option::Some(Lendable::Down) => false,
    Option::None => false,
  }
}

fn mark_idle(got: Option<Lendable>) -> () {
  match got {
    Option::Some(Lendable::Ready(loan)) => Shared::set(loan.here, true),
    Option::Some(Lendable::Down) => (),
    Option::None => (),
  }
}

/// The offer out of its `Option`, consuming it, so the offer is the only
/// thing holding the loan by the time it is sent. The caller checked it is
/// there.
fn the_offer(got: Option<Lendable>) -> Lendable {
  match got {
    Option::Some(offer) => offer,
    // Not reached; see above.
    Option::None => Lendable::Down,
  }
}

/// Sends a connection to its slot, which reads any reply still arriving on
/// it and then lends it again or closes it. The token has no slot and is
/// dropped.
///
/// **The offer is passed whole, and only its home is looked up**, so the
/// send's argument is this function's own parameter at its last use and
/// nothing else holds it: a loan taken out of a `match` arm would still be
/// held by the value matched on, and the send would trap.
fn send_home(offer: Lendable) -> () {
  match home_of(offer) {
    Option::None => (),
    Option::Some(home) => { Handoff::send(home, offer); () },
  }
}

fn home_of(offer: Lendable) -> Option<Handoff<Lendable>> {
  match offer {
    Lendable::Ready(loan) => Option::Some(loan.home),
    Lendable::Down => Option::None,
  }
}

// --- the slot's fiber --------------------------------------------------------

/// One slot, for the pool's whole life: connect, lend, reconnect.
fn slot(
  settings: Settings,
  plan: Reconnect,
  idle: Handoff<Lendable>,
  token_idle: Shared<Bool>,
  here: Shared<Bool>,
  tally: Shared<Tally>,
) -> () {
  // Room for the one connection this slot owns.
  let home: Handoff<Lendable> = Handoff::bounded(1);
  // **Counted out however this fiber ends**, so a slot stopped by its crew
  // still lets `close` see it has finished.
  let done = Region::open();
  Region::defer(done, finisher(tally));
  with { clock: Clock::real(), random: Random::real() } {
    let tries: Tries = { began: clock.monotonic_millis(), attempts: 0, phase: Phase::Reconnecting };
    let mut going = true;
    while going {
      let got = reconnect(settings, plan, idle, token_idle, tally, tries);
      if is_none(got) {
        going = false
      } else {
        let opened = clock.monotonic_millis();
        // Taken out of its `Option` by a call, so nothing in this frame
        // still holds the connection when `lend` gives it away.
        match lend(offer_of(got, home, here), home, here, idle, tally) {
          Ended::Closing => going = false,
          Ended::Served => {
            moved(tally, idle, token_idle, Phase::Live, Phase::Reconnecting);
            tries.began = clock.monotonic_millis();
            tries.attempts = 0;
            tries.phase = Phase::Reconnecting
          },
          Ended::Spoiled => {
            // **The time it sat open is not time spent reconnecting.** A
            // connection nobody leased for longer than the fast phase, then
            // closed by a server restart, would otherwise find the phase
            // already over and take its slot straight to `down`.
            tries.began = tries.began + (clock.monotonic_millis() - opened);
            moved(tally, idle, token_idle, Phase::Live, tries.phase);
            remember(tally, spoiled());
            if !back_off(plan, idle, token_idle, tally, tries) { going = false }
          },
        }
      }
    };
  };
}

fn is_none<A>(o: Option<A>) -> Bool {
  match o { Option::None => true, Option::Some(_) => false }
}

/// The connection out of `got`, as an offer, consuming `got`: by the time
/// this returns, the offer is the only thing holding the connection.
fn offer_of(got: Option<Connection>, home: Handoff<Lendable>, here: Shared<Bool>) -> Lendable {
  match got {
    Option::Some(c) => Lendable::Ready({ c: c, home: home, here: here, served: false }),
    // Not reached: `slot` looked first.
    Option::None => Lendable::Down,
  }
}

/// The slot's end, built in a function that calls no function value, so it
/// has no entry poll for a cancellation to stop it at.
fn finisher(tally: Shared<Tally>) -> () -> () {
  fn () => finished(tally)
}

fn finished(tally: Shared<Tally>) -> () {
  Shared::update(tally, fn t => {
      live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down, last: t.last,
      closing: t.closing, token: t.token, running: t.running - 1,
    });
  ()
}

fn spoiled() -> DbError {
  DbError::Disconnected("the connection failed its check before it could be lent")
}

/// Puts a connection into `idle`, and each time it comes home, either lends
/// it again or closes it and says why: the pool is closing, or it broke,
/// after or before any lease on it passed its check.
///
/// **A connection that comes home with a reply still arriving is read to the
/// end of it here**, and lent again if that leaves it healthy. Its borrower
/// was stopped while it waited -- a transaction canceled in its `ROLLBACK`,
/// say -- and a stopped borrower cannot wait for the rest; this fiber can,
/// because nothing stops it but `close`. Closing it instead would cost a
/// reconnect per stopped borrower. What it costs: while the server is still
/// sending, the slot waits, and so does a `close` that waits for the slot.
///
/// **The connection is closed here, by the slot, and only here**, and never
/// read again: whatever the server was still sending on it goes with it.
fn lend(first: Lendable, home: Handoff<Lendable>, here: Shared<Bool>, idle: Handoff<Lendable>, tally: Shared<Tally>) -> Ended {
  Shared::set(here, true);
  Handoff::send(idle, first);
  let mut result: Option<Ended> = Option::None;
  while is_none(result) {
    let got = Handoff::receive(home);
    match returned(got, tally) {
      Option::None => {
        Shared::set(here, true);
        Handoff::send(idle, the_offer(got));
        ()
      },
      Option::Some(why) => {
        hang_up_offer(got);
        result = Option::Some(why)
      },
    }
  };
  match result {
    Option::Some(why) => why,
    // Not reached: the loop leaves only with an answer.
    Option::None => Ended::Closing,
  }
}

/// What a connection that came home means, once any reply still arriving on
/// it has been read: `None` to lend it again, or why its slot closes it.
fn returned(got: Option<Lendable>, tally: Shared<Tally>) -> Option<Ended> {
  match got {
    Option::Some(Lendable::Ready(loan)) => {
      let _ = settle(loan.c);
      let fine = usable(loan.c) && !in_transaction(loan.c);
      if fine && !closing(tally) {
        Option::None
      } else if fine {
        Option::Some(Ended::Closing)
      } else if loan.served {
        Option::Some(Ended::Served)
      } else {
        Option::Some(Ended::Spoiled)
      }
    },
    // Not reached: only connections are sent home, and `home` is never
    // closed.
    Option::Some(Lendable::Down) => Option::Some(Ended::Closing),
    Option::None => Option::Some(Ended::Closing),
  }
}

fn hang_up_offer(got: Option<Lendable>) -> () {
  match got {
    Option::Some(Lendable::Ready(loan)) => hang_up(loan.c),
    Option::Some(Lendable::Down) => (),
    Option::None => (),
  }
}

/// Connects, on the fast schedule and then the slow interval. `None` when the
/// pool closes first, or when the slot shrinks for good.
fn reconnect(
  settings: Settings,
  plan: Reconnect,
  idle: Handoff<Lendable>,
  token_idle: Shared<Bool>,
  tally: Shared<Tally>,
  tries: Tries,
) -> Option<Connection>
  with { clock: Clock, random: Random }
{
  let mut outcome: Option<Connection> = Option::None;
  let mut going = true;
  while going {
    if closing(tally) {
      going = false
    } else {
      match connect(settings, plan.handshake) {
        Result::Ok(c) => {
          if closing(tally) {
            // Opened just as the pool closed: nobody will lend it.
            hang_up(c)
          } else {
            outcome = Option::Some(c)
          };
          going = false
        },
        Result::Err(why) => {
          remember(tally, why);
          if !back_off(plan, idle, token_idle, tally, tries) { going = false }
        },
      }
    }
  };
  match outcome {
    Option::Some(c) => {
      moved(tally, idle, token_idle, tries.phase, Phase::Live);
      Option::Some(c)
    },
    Option::None => Option::None,
  }
}

/// Counts one failed attempt and waits as long as the schedule says: the
/// fast phase's next delay, or, once that has given up, the slow interval
/// with the slot moved to `down`. `false` when the slot should stop: the
/// pool closed during the wait, or the shrink is for good.
///
/// The one place a failure is counted, for a connect that failed and a
/// connection that failed its check alike, so the two cannot drift apart.
fn back_off(
  plan: Reconnect,
  idle: Handoff<Lendable>,
  token_idle: Shared<Bool>,
  tally: Shared<Tally>,
  tries: Tries,
) -> Bool
  with { clock: Clock, random: Random }
{
  tries.attempts = tries.attempts + 1;
  let wait = match tries.phase {
    Phase::Down => slow_wait(plan),
    // Not reached: a slot only backs off from `Reconnecting` or `Down`.
    // Stopping is the answer that cannot loop.
    Phase::Live => Option::None,
    Phase::Reconnecting => {
      let now = clock.monotonic_millis() - tries.began;
      match Schedule::next_after(plan.fast, tries.attempts, now) {
        Option::Some(at) => Option::Some(at - now),
        Option::None => {
          tries.phase = Phase::Down;
          moved(tally, idle, token_idle, Phase::Reconnecting, Phase::Down);
          slow_wait(plan)
        },
      }
    },
  };
  match wait {
    Option::None => false,
    Option::Some(millis) => pause(tally, millis),
  }
}

/// The slow interval with 80-120% jitter, or `None` for a permanent shrink.
fn slow_wait(plan: Reconnect) -> Option<Int> with { random: Random } {
  match plan.slow {
    Option::None => Option::None,
    Option::Some(every) => Option::Some(every * random.in_range(80, 121) / 100),
  }
}

/// How long one slice of a wait is.
///
/// **What this bounds: how long `close` waits for a slot that is between
/// attempts**, and how often `close` looks at the pool while a lease it is
/// waiting for is out. The runtime has no receive with a deadline, so
/// neither can sleep on something that would wake it; each sleeps in slices
/// and looks between them. The cost is a wake every slice per waiting fiber.
fn slice() -> Int { 25 }

/// Waits `millis`, or less if the pool closes. `false` if it closed.
fn pause(tally: Shared<Tally>, millis: Int) -> Bool with { clock: Clock } {
  let until = clock.monotonic_millis() + millis;
  let mut open = true;
  let mut waiting = true;
  while waiting {
    let left = until - clock.monotonic_millis();
    if closing(tally) {
      open = false;
      waiting = false
    } else if left <= 0 {
      waiting = false
    } else {
      clock.sleep(if left < slice() { left } else { slice() })
    }
  };
  open
}

fn closing(tally: Shared<Tally>) -> Bool {
  Shared::get(tally).closing
}

/// Keeps `why` as the reason a pool with nothing live gives its callers.
fn remember(tally: Shared<Tally>, why: DbError) -> () {
  let text = match why {
    DbError::Disconnected(m) => m,
    DbError::Rejected(m) => m,
    DbError::RolledBack(m) => m,
  };
  Shared::update(tally, fn t => {
      live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down, last: text,
      closing: t.closing, token: t.token, running: t.running,
    });
}

/// Moves one slot from `from` to `to`, and puts the down token into `idle`
/// when that leaves nothing live or reconnecting.
fn moved(tally: Shared<Tally>, idle: Handoff<Lendable>, token_idle: Shared<Bool>, from: Phase, to: Phase) -> () {
  let token_now = Shared::modify(tally, fn t => {
      let live = t.live + step(Phase::Live, from, to);
      let reconnecting = t.reconnecting + step(Phase::Reconnecting, from, to);
      let down = t.down + step(Phase::Down, from, to);
      let wanted = !t.closing && !t.token && live + reconnecting == 0;
      {
        state: { live: live, reconnecting: reconnecting, down: down, last: t.last,
          closing: t.closing, token: t.token || wanted, running: t.running },
        result: wanted,
      }
    });
  if token_now {
    Shared::set(token_idle, true);
    Handoff::send(idle, Lendable::Down);
    ()
  };
}

/// What moving a slot from `from` to `to` does to the count of `which`.
fn step(which: Phase, from: Phase, to: Phase) -> Int {
  (if same(which, to) { 1 } else { 0 }) - (if same(which, from) { 1 } else { 0 })
}

fn same(a: Phase, b: Phase) -> Bool {
  match a {
    Phase::Live => match b { Phase::Live => true, Phase::Reconnecting => false, Phase::Down => false },
    Phase::Reconnecting =>
      match b { Phase::Live => false, Phase::Reconnecting => true, Phase::Down => false },
    Phase::Down => match b { Phase::Live => false, Phase::Reconnecting => false, Phase::Down => true },
  }
}

// --- closing -----------------------------------------------------------------

/// Stops every slot and waits for them.
///
/// **Every connection in `idle` is taken and sent home to be closed, and every
/// caller waiting in `with_db` is answered.** The pool is marked closing and
/// the down token put into `idle` if it is not out already; a waiter that
/// receives it answers "the pool is closed" and its give-back passes it on,
/// and a lease that ends sends its connection home rather than to `idle`.
/// `close` takes what it finds in `idle` until every slot has finished,
/// putting the token back each time it takes it, and a slot between attempts
/// sees the close within one `slice`. A connection somebody is using is not
/// closed underneath them: its lease ends when their `with_db` returns.
///
/// What it costs while a lease is out: a turn of the token every `slice`.
/// What it cannot cut short: a connection attempt already under way. One in
/// the startup exchange ends within the plan's `handshake` bound, when its
/// read gives up; one still inside the operating system's TCP `connect` ends
/// when that does, which for an address that drops packets is the system's
/// connect timeout -- minutes on Linux.
pub fn close(pool: Pool) -> () {
  let put = Shared::modify(pool.tally, fn t => {
      state: { live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down, last: t.last,
        closing: true, token: true, running: t.running },
      result: !t.token,
    });
  if put {
    Shared::set(pool.token_idle, true);
    Handoff::send(pool.idle, Lendable::Down);
    ()
  };
  with { clock: Clock::real() } {
    while Shared::get(pool.tally).running > 0 {
      let held = holding(Handoff::receive(pool.idle));
      match what(held, pool.token_idle) {
        // Not reached: `idle` is never closed.
        Offered::Nothing => clock.sleep(slice()),
        Offered::Token => {
          put_token_back(pool, held);
          clock.sleep(slice())
        },
        Offered::Lent => send_home(the_offer(take_out(held))),
      }
    };
  };
  // **The count is discarded here, and only here.** `Fibers::wait` answers
  // how many children ended with an error, and this is a shutdown: the slots
  // were stopped on purpose above. A pool that raised on the way out would be
  // reporting its own request to stop as a fault, and there is nobody left
  // to tell.
  let _stopped = Fibers::wait(pool.crew);
}

fn put_token_back(pool: Pool, held: Held) -> () {
  let _ = take_out(held);
  Shared::set(pool.token_idle, true);
  Handoff::send(pool.idle, Lendable::Down);
  ()
}
